---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
const essay = 'https://bhuvan.substack.com/p/between-hope-and-despair';
---
<BaseLayout title="About — Hope & Despair" description="Why this atlas exists, who makes it, and how — including, plainly, the part AI plays.">
  <article class="about">
    <header class="head">
      <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
      <h1>I could not decide whether the world was getting better or worse. So I built this instead of deciding.</h1>
    </header>

    <section class="s">
      <p class="lead">I’m Bhuvan. I work in finance, and for the better part of a decade I went back and forth on
        the same question without ever landing. This atlas is what came out of not landing.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="s">
      <h2>Where it started</h2>
      <p>Around 2017 I read Steven Pinker and the long-run optimists — the charts where almost everything you
        can count, from child mortality to literacy to extreme poverty, has bent the right way for two centuries.
        It should have settled the question for me. It didn’t. I had spent my life assuming I was a pessimist,
        and then I noticed something awkward: I <em>invest</em> like an optimist. I put money to work on the bet
        that things compound and improve. My self-image said one thing; what I actually did with my savings said
        the opposite. That gap is where this began.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="s">
      <h2>Two true stories</h2>
      <p>One story is the optimist’s. The economist Joel Mokyr puts it about as strongly as it can be put:
        a person near the bottom of the ladder today lives better, in almost every measurable dimension, than the
        popes and emperors of the past. By the numbers, he is right.</p>
      <p>The other story belongs to people like Daniel Schmachtenberger: we improve on the handful of metrics we
        choose to measure and optimise for, and quietly lose ground on the ones we don’t — soils, oceans,
        attention, trust, the odds of a genuinely bad century. By a different set of numbers, he is also right.</p>
      <p>Both cases are made by serious people with real evidence. The mistake I kept making was assuming one of
        them had to win.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="s">
      <h2>Hope is not optimism</h2>
      <p>The line that finally unstuck me came from Cory Doctorow. An optimist, he says, doesn’t bother to equip
        the Titanic with lifeboats, because the ship cannot sink. Optimism and pessimism are both just
        predictions — and a prediction tells you to sit still, because the ending is already written. Hope is a
        different thing. Hope is what you do while the outcome is still open. “Hope is the necessary, but
        insufficient, precondition for survival.” That stance — not the prediction, but the practice — is the one
        this site tries to take.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="s">
      <h2>What this is</h2>
      <p>A living atlas. It asks one honest question at a time — <em>is the world winning against poverty? is it
        becoming less violent? is the climate stabilising or breaking?</em> — and answers it with open, cited data
        and plain writing, nothing else. Each question is read at every magnification, from the ten-thousand-year
        arc down to a single lived day, because the answer usually changes with the distance you stand at. And
        every reading comes in three temperatures: hope, despair, and confusion — the last of which gets equal
        billing here rather than a footnote.</p>
      <p>Underneath all of it is one idea: progress and catastrophe are both real, at the same time. The work
        isn’t to pick a side. It’s to be honest about which one you’re looking at, and through which lens. There’s
        a <a href={essay} rel="noopener" target="_blank">longer essay</a> on how I got here, if you want the full
        version.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="s">
      <h2>Who makes this</h2>
      <p>My name is Bhuvan. I work in finance, and in my own time I build small, one-person websites that try to
        drag useful things out of wherever they are buried and set them down in plain language. This is one of them.</p>
      <p>This one has a parent. <a href="https://thisindianlife.today" rel="noopener" target="_blank">This
        Indian Life</a> does the same job for a single country — it hauls the most important numbers about life in
        India out of horrible government PDFs and sets them down in plain language. Hope &amp; Despair is that same
        instinct turned on the whole world: the question got bigger, the method stayed the same. A few other sites
        lean the same way — <a href="https://akshara.ink" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Akshara</a> digitises
        public-domain literature and history;
        <a href="https://seneca.ink" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Seneca</a>,
        <a href="https://smallweb.blog" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Smallweb</a>,
        <a href="https://tipsheet.markets" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Tipsheet</a>,
        <a href="https://paperlanterns.ink" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Paper Lanterns</a> — each a small
        attempt at one useful thing.</p>
      <p>I am not a developer by training; I build these with the help of large language models, which is the only
        reason one person can attempt them at all. I’m strongest on economics and finance and genuinely shaky on a
        great deal outside it, so where this atlas wanders past what I know, it leans hard on cited sources and says
        plainly when it is unsure. You can reach me at <a href="mailto:bhuvan@bebhuvan.com">bhuvan@bebhuvan.com</a>
        or <a href="https://twitter.com/bebhuvan" rel="noopener" target="_blank">@bebhuvan</a>.</p>
    </section>

    <aside class="disclosure" aria-label="How this is made">
      <svg class="dmark" viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true">
        <path d="M8 1.6 a6.4 6.4 0 0 0 0 12.8 z" fill="var(--hope)" />
        <path d="M8 1.6 a6.4 6.4 0 0 1 0 12.8 z" fill="var(--despair)" />
        <line x1="8" y1="1.2" x2="8" y2="14.8" stroke="var(--bg)" stroke-width="0.8" />
        <circle cx="8" cy="8" r="6.4" fill="none" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="0.9" />
      </svg>
      <div class="dbody">
        <p class="dl">How it is made — including the AI part</p>
        <p class="dt"><b>This is a one-person project, made with AI assistance, and I’d rather state that plainly
          than bury it.</b> One person cannot research, write, fact-check, and keep hundreds of questions like
          these current by hand — it simply isn’t possible. So the prose is drafted with a large language model
          working only from data I have already ingested and cited, and then I read it, check it, and edit it.</p>
        <p class="dt">The numbers are never the model’s invention. Every figure traces to a named, openly-licensed
          source; the data and its full lineage are downloadable under each chart. Where the evidence is thin or a
          verdict is a judgement call, the writing says so. Every change is tracked in public, commit by commit.</p>
      </div>
    </aside>

    <section class="s">
      <h2>License &amp; reuse</h2>
      <p>The original prose, code, and chart designs are released under
        <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">CC&nbsp;BY&nbsp;4.0&nbsp;↗</a>
        — reuse them freely with attribution to hopeanddespair.world. The source datasets keep their own licences
        (usually CC&nbsp;BY), credited per article with licence and vintage. Where a source doesn’t allow
        re-hosting, I link to it rather than copying its numbers. The <a href="/license">full terms are here</a>.</p>
    </section>

    <p class="back"><a href="/">← Back to the atlas</a></p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .about { max-width: 42rem; padding-block: clamp(3rem, 8vh, 5.5rem) 2rem; }
  .head .eyebrow { font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 0.74rem; letter-spacing: 0.06em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--ink-3); }
  .head h1 { font-family: var(--display); font-weight: 620; font-size: clamp(2rem, 4.4vw, 3.2rem); line-height: 1.08; letter-spacing: -0.032em; margin-top: 1.1rem; max-width: 20ch; text-wrap: balance; }

  .s { margin-top: clamp(2.2rem, 5vh, 3.2rem); }
  .s h2 { font-family: var(--display); font-weight: 580; font-size: 1.34rem; letter-spacing: -0.02em; color: var(--ink); margin-bottom: 0.9rem; }
  .lead { font-family: var(--serif); font-size: var(--fs-lede); line-height: 1.5; color: var(--ink); text-wrap: pretty; }
  .s p { max-width: var(--measure); font-size: var(--fs-body); line-height: var(--lh-body); color: var(--ink-2); font-weight: 450; text-wrap: pretty; }
  .s p + p { margin-top: 0.95rem; }
  .s p em { font-style: italic; color: var(--ink); }
  .s a { color: var(--ink); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line-2); }
  .s a:hover { color: var(--hope); border-color: var(--hope); }

  /* the AI disclosure — prominent, the split mark beside it, never fine print */
  .disclosure { margin-top: clamp(2.8rem, 7vh, 4.4rem); padding-top: clamp(1.8rem, 4.5vh, 2.6rem); border-top: 1px solid var(--line-2); display: grid; grid-template-columns: auto 1fr; gap: 1.2rem; align-items: start; }
  .disclosure .dmark { width: 1.6rem; height: 1.6rem; margin-top: 0.3rem; }
  .disclosure .dl { font-family: var(--display); font-weight: 600; font-size: 0.84rem; letter-spacing: 0.02em; color: var(--ink-3); margin-bottom: 0.8rem; }
  .disclosure .dt { max-width: var(--measure); font-size: var(--fs-body); line-height: var(--lh-body); color: var(--ink-2); font-weight: 450; text-wrap: pretty; }
  .disclosure .dt + .dt { margin-top: 0.9rem; }
  .disclosure .dt :global(b) { font-weight: 600; color: var(--ink); }
  @media (max-width: 560px) { .disclosure { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 0.8rem; } }

  .back { margin-top: clamp(2.6rem, 6vh, 3.6rem); font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 0.74rem; }
  .back a { color: var(--ink-2); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line-2); }
  .back a:hover { color: var(--hope); border-color: var(--hope); }
</style>
